Here are the three TUnit skip patterns:

**1. Skip a specific test method (compile-time)**

```csharp
[Test]
[Skip("Waiting on payment-gateway sandbox provisioning")]
public async Task ChargeCard_ReturnsSuccess()
{
    // ...
}
```

**2. Skip every test in an assembly (conditional on configuration)**

```csharp
// In a top-level file (e.g., AssemblyAttributes.cs)
#if SMOKE
[assembly: Skip("Assembly skipped: smoke configuration — only critical path tests run")]
#endif
```

Build with `-p:DefineConstants=SMOKE` to activate the skip.

**3. Runtime skip inside a test method**

```csharp
[Test]
public async Task SyncAdGroup_UpdatesMembership()
{
    if (!IsConnectedToCorpVpn())
    {
        Assert.Skip("Machine is not joined to the corporate VPN");
    }

    // ... rest of test
}

private static bool IsConnectedToCorpVpn()
{
    // e.g., attempt to resolve an internal DNS name
    try
    {
        Dns.GetHostEntry("internal.corp.example.com");
        return true;
    }
    catch
    {
        return false;
    }
}
```

---

| Scenario | Mechanism | Granularity |
|----------|-----------|-------------|
| Known blocker | `[Skip("reason")]` on method | Single test |
| Build configuration | `[assembly: Skip("reason")]` with `#if` | Entire assembly |
| Runtime condition | `Assert.Skip("reason")` | Mid-test bail-out |

All three report the test as **skipped** (not failed) in the test results, preserving the reason string in the output.
